[Music]  hi this is mario aquino i'm one of the  organizers for the strange loop  conference  and this is another episode of strange  loop chats with me today is paula guerin  she is an engineer at cisco in the  threat grid group  and we're going to be talking today  about an open source database that paula  maintains it's called asami  before i get to that i'd like to mention  that strange loop this year is going to  start on thursday september 30th which  is going to be our workshop day  and the conference proper is going to  start on friday october 1st and run two  days  the 1st and 2nd of october if you'd like  to find out more  you can visit thestrangeloop.com for  details about  tickets and the schedule so with that  out of the way  let me introduce paula paula thank you  for  sitting with me for this interview  welcome  thanks mario so i wanted to talk to you  today  about asami what can you tell me about  asami what is it  it's a graph database and it started  life  as a in memory graph database but  recently we've developed it into  something which also  stores persistently on disk and looking  at different types of provisioning for  being able to store it in lots of  different ways and locations as well but  yeah it's a  graph database written entirely in  closure and it  is there to provide graph services for  closure developers who want to use data  in that way  so can you describe what exactly is a  graph database  the graph database represents graph data  where a graph  in the mathematical sense is essentially  like a network  it looks like a whole series of nodes  with lines  uh connecting so we typically think of  these things as each node  each start representing some kind of  resource and then the line in between  will have a label and it will be  typically in a direction so if i have a  resource  which represents a person i can have  lines in which go off to the different  attributes of the person like their name  their address  and things like that and i can also have  lines which connect  that person's node to nodes which  represent other people either as friends  or their manager  or neighbors then we can have nodes  which represent  companies or cities or anything at all  really  and so it's a very flexible arrangement  for data  and it provides this really consistent  way of looking at any kind of data at  all  so it's a database that allows you to  establish relationships between entities  that you store in it and it's written in  closure  is it a database that also works in  closure script originally i wrote it as  closure and then i was asked can you  move it up into the browser so we did  our principal use for it at  cisco at the moment is in the browser  and it's  operating as a memory model for an  important product that we've got which  has a lot of deployment  internationally now but it also works on  a machine  running on the jvm and it's in that  state at the moment that we've been  working  a lot with the persistence model for how  this can be stored  effectively so the name asami  can you tell me what is that sort of an  internal joke  um five years ago we  uh started with this project which was  the  uh internet resource orchestration hub  ira  or uncle iro was a character in the tv  series  avatar the last fair bender and our  manager at the time  really enjoyed that show and he started  naming all these all of these projects  after  avatar character this i think ira  running in the cloud was called  tenzin which is from the avatar spin-off  well asami  is a character from cora as well  originally i  had written a rules engine which  used asani and that engine was called  naga  again another character from the same tv  show  so what would you say distinguishes  asami from  other graph databases well  every graph database has its own pros  and cons i mean originally asami was  simply  an in-memory model for naga because we  wanted something  which where we wouldn't need an external  dependency  i was building it around daytonic and  because we were going to be doing this  thing in-house i was asked well  could you maybe do something that  doesn't involve a commercial system with  that way this whole thing could be open  source and  i if i had thought i was going to build  an entire graph database i'd have said  none  we weren't aware of data script at that  point i built the basic functionality  that was required because that's all we  needed  but over time as naga needed to do more  things with rules  we introduced more and more features and  finally i realized that  this storage system had gained a life of  its own  and we broke it out into its own project  and now it's become  dominant work for me where the the whole  query engine the query language  everything's been expanding and you know  from that point it then  i was asked to move it into the browser  and so it became a cross-platform  project between  jvm and enclosure script in the browser  so yeah running our own project that way  did give us the flexibility to make that  move  and it's also given us the opportunity  to introduce new features as we wanted  them  things like aggregates which you know  daytonic already had  but also things like graph analytics so  we can do  queries on transitive attributes or we  can find  subgrants where if you've got two  distinct  disconnected graphs we can identify  those  and you know different sorts of graph  algorithms like this  are all things we've been able to  implement internally so that they're  really  optimized for the data structures you  mentioned that asami has  querying capability you know you can't  really have a  database without a querying capability  what would you say inspired the way that  the querying works in asami  two things um firstly daytona  so daytonic has specified a graph  querying language  which is written in using the data  structures that make up closure itself  so essentially eden and  that's been really useful to leverage  because we can read it for free we don't  have to worry about parsing  but the other thing that it's based on  is sparkle so  20 years ago i was a lead architect  on another graph database called moldara  and that was written entirely for the  jvm  back then we developed our own query  language and  over time it became clear that idf data  required its own  query language a standard and so a  committee was formed which was the  spargle committee  i wasn't on that one i contributed to it  but then  some years later as new features were  required the second cycle committee was  put together and i was on that i was one  of the editors for  the documents so there's a whole lot of  semantics in sparkle which  differ a little bit from daytonic  and because of some of the architectural  decisions that we've made in asami it  made much more sense to go  often with the sparkle semantics over  the the daytona genetics  so the syntax is is some kind of a  a union between atomic query language  and  sparkle semantics mostly in the edge  cases  in most queries you're going to find  that you can copy paste something from  daytona or datascript and just use it on  asami and it'll just work  but there are a few uh a few things  where you're going to find  like doing a not operation or certain  kinds of unions  where the edge cases lean more towards  the sparkle  interpretation of the world rather than  daytona  so what would you say is sort of uh  an ideal use case for for asami where  does it really shine  when you've got a lot of disparate data  so a lot of data that you're getting  from different places where you want to  find linkages between the data where you  want to  take data which may have come from  tables but when you put it together you  would  otherwise require dozens and dozens of  tables with lots of foreign joins and  things like that  if it were in the relational world when  you're bringing a lot of  data together like that then graph  databases in general are very useful  that way asami  has been designed internally to be  optimized around querying  it's optimized around querying but you  know at the expense of taking a little  longer to load data around  so if you're going to be doing as  someone mentioned yesterday  30 transactions a second then  assam is going to be slower than but if  you're only  you know if you're loading your data up  once or every few minutes  then and querying it a lot and analyzing  it a lot then assam is really going to  show  and you know assignment can still handle  a lot of transactions  but there are other systems which are  going to be optimized more around that  and yet they won't necessarily answer  queries as quickly  you're actively maintaining asami what  is it that you're  sort of focusing on right now in terms  of the feature set that you're trying to  bring to it  i'm focusing on what my colleagues at  work say they need  so when someone says like can i do this  or doesn't that  you know does this work the way i think  it should then  that's my main priority but when people  aren't asking me for that i've been  working  on storage with just coming up to  a 2.0 release which is where the the  durable storage they're not entirely  confined to memory anymore  is finally going to be released there's  more architecture to happen there there  are  certain elements of it which i'm really  happy with and other things which i know  could uh use more effective data  structures  but it works really well at the moment  and i'm looking forward to just getting  that out the door  and then we've got more more layers of  indexing  and and features that we want to have  there you know  there's trade-offs at every level but  looking at and introducing some of these  new features  to meet the the requirements that my  colleagues have frankly to  you know to speed up certain things and  to make data available easily  in in some ways where it may not be so  easy  to get to it at the moment so in terms  of  durable storage what is the sort of goal  for being able to to persist  the database i guess in in its in the  in a format that has already some some  amount of entity data  in it is it for replication is it for  quick loading like a database that  already  you know contains so everything's  immutable internally  and i have a whole lot of documentation  around this  on the on the project website and it you  know internally  the uh closure itself uses these kinds  of data structures  the last database i was working on  moldara it also used  that kind of data structure and because  we have a whole lot of immutable data  structures  so things which never change we only add  to the storage  this means that replication is really  quite easy  we can just take things knowing that  they're never going to change again  we can then spread it across the cluster  because  again we know that once something's been  written it never changes  and this gives us a whole lot of you  know advantages almost for free  one of the one of the things which you  know when daytonic came out it seemed  relevant  like a revelation was that  you could go back in time and look at  how things used to be ironically mulgara  always had that kind of data structure  but  we built it 20 years ago when disc  wasn't as cheap as it is now  and consequently we needed to clean up  any data that was old that we weren't  using anymore and  you know for performance reasons over  time i eventually ripped that  that code out because all that  bookkeeping and cleanliness was  you know had a cost for the database and  so i was just leaving these things  hanging around  and then when daytonic came out and we  were shown that we had this rich history  of what all the data would look like  getting to this point  i realized at that point that mulgara  already had that but it just hadn't  exposed it to the user asami has that  now  and it's been inspired by my by  muldering structures  i've modernized them i updated things  over the years as i came up with better  ideas as to how these things should be  built  and yeah so a number of the features  which  which daytonic has which make it like  very different to  other sorts of databases before uh are  also present  in asani and you know i kind of  while i'm not aiming to do exactly the  same sorts of things as  daytonic at the same time it has led me  to certain features thinking you know i  kind of like that i'd like to have my  have that in my system as well and sure  enough people  you know for the things i haven't  finished doing people come to me and say  when do we get this feature so i know  that it's wanted community wise  there are i guess groups outside of  cisco  that are either using or experimenting  with asami  so a friend of mine started a channel on  the closure in slack  and a number of people have been joining  it i've seen people saying that they  assessing the sami for their workplace  i've had feedback i've had  full requests to you know to create new  features  or to expand on things and so  there seems to be a lot of interest  about what i'm doing which is  really quite nice and you know i want to  be responsive to  the people who are using it as well yeah  i mean this was kind of a hobby of mine  that i was doing on the side and i  showed my manager years ago and he said  this looks great do it for work  and you know so i'm i'm in a really  privileged position that i get to do  something which i was finding  interesting  for my job and you know i i want to be  as responsive to people as i can be so  that i get to keep doing this job so  for folks interested in checking out  asami for themselves  where should they go to github it's  under threat grid asami it's an open  source project all the documentation's  online  i do work hard to try to make sure that  everything is really well documented  a lot of times people ask me for how  things are working and i'm able to point  them at docs  and that's really paid for itself so  hopefully if you need to find things  then you can go there otherwise  you can always go to the clojurian slack  and look for asami  if you ask questions i do try to be as  responsive  as i can be all right well  paula i want to thank you for sitting  down for this  strange loop chat and for folks who are  watching if you would like to find out  more about asami there will be  links when this video is posted to where  you can read about it  on github well paula thanks again it's  been great talking to you  you're welcome thanks maureen  [Music]  you

